\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{The covariant lattice Dirac operator}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{How does a fermion move through a fixed gauge connection?
The [spatial Dirac operator](fdip-000R) combines centered differences
with Clifford matrices. The [connection background](fdip-006D)
compares fields at neighboring vertices by parallel transport.
Combining them requires two separate factors: a Clifford module
#{S} and an internal representation #{F}. The gauge links act on
#{F}, while the gamma matrices act on #{S}.}

\p{This separation makes the operator gauge covariant and
skew-adjoint. It also identifies its finite matrix as a possible
kernel for the [paired Grassmann integral](fdip-008H), without
claiming a positive probability density. At the trivial connection,
the Fourier blocks retain the centered difference's sine symbol.
Their exact zero modes depend on the parity of the finite periods;
the extra local branches of the continuous momentum symbol are a
different count. Fixed antiperiodic boundary conditions change the
sampling but do not remove those branches.}

\p{A group acting on spin frames has a different role from an
internal gauge group. Even when both are described using
#{SU(2)} or a binary rotation group, their actions and the
Clifford compatibility must be supplied separately.}

